{"reference":"1 Samuel 10","book":"1 Samuel","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Kingship and accountability","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"This chapter presents Saul’s kingship as granted by the LORD: Samuel anoints him, God empowers him, and the people are shown the one the LORD has chosen. At the same time, the account does not treat having a king as freedom from obligation; monarchy remains accountable to fearing, serving, and obeying the LORD.","verse_refs":["1 Samuel 10:1-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_010_c07d7c7f853c147c17d911ad"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The wider setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Read within 1 Samuel 8–12, Saul’s selection is held alongside the book’s severe criticism of Israel’s request for a king. The larger account therefore presents monarchy as permitted and accountable within Israel’s covenant life, rather than as simply good or simply evil.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_samuel_010_c23f0b3927b6449acc82be8a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}